"Surah an-Nisa begins by addressing humanity and deals with important matters such as women, orphans, marriage, and inheritance. In the first verse, emphasizing that human beings were created from a single soul, it is commanded to be mindful of Allah and beware of disobeying Him. While this draws attention to the brotherhood of humanity and the unity of creation, it also reminds that the rights of kinship must be observed.\n\nThe evidence of creation is emphasized; attributing everything to nature is incorrect, because nature cannot explain the emergence of female from male. By Allah's power, Adam was created from earth, and Eve was created from Adam. Marriage and the multiplication of progeny are encouraged; this is a divine discipline and a duty of servanthood.\n\nThe creation of Adam and Eve is explained through the hadith regarding creation from the rib. This shows the distinct natures of man and woman and the harmony between them. The concept of 'rahim' (kinship) is emphasized; observing the rights of relatives and maintaining family ties holds great importance in the sight of Allah. Numerous hadiths are narrated on this subject.\n\nThe preservation and protection of orphans' property is commanded. Mixing the property of orphans with one's own or consuming it unjustly is a grave sin. The word 'orphan' (yatim), in addition to children whose fathers have died, also encompasses widowed women. It is demanded that orphans be treated with justice and that their property be handed over to them fully and on time.\n\nThe verse regarding polygyny states that if one fears acting unjustly when marrying orphan girls, one may marry other women of one's choice, two, three, or four, but that justice is a strict condition. If justice cannot be maintained, one must be content with a single woman or a bondwoman. According to the tafsir of Aisha, this verse was revealed to prevent guardians from committing injustice against their orphan wards.\n\nThere are various narrations regarding the occasion of revelation of this verse. According to a narration from Ibn Abbas, during the pre-Islamic period of ignorance (Jahiliyyah), men would marry orphan girls out of greed for their wealth and would not observe their rights. With this verse, Allah Almighty commanded that those who fear acting unjustly toward the wealth of orphans must also observe justice in marrying women and marry no more than four. This is, at the same time, a restriction on marrying more than one woman.\n\nAnother narration comes from Hasan al-Basri: Guardians would marry orphan girls for their wealth, mistreat them, and wait for their deaths. This verse prohibited them from this attitude. Furthermore, according to a narration from Ikrima, a man would marry a large number of women, and when his wealth was exhausted, he would turn to the wealth of orphans. For this reason, marrying more than four women was prohibited.\n\nSome commentators interpreted the occasion of revelation of the verse differently. According to Mujahid, it means: \"If you fear committing injustice regarding orphans, fear committing unlawful sexual intercourse (zina) as well.\" Emphasizing the moral dimension of the verse, this interpretation states that the permission to marry more than one woman is a safeguard against zina. Aisha's narration, on the other hand, indicates the clearest reason: Guardians would prevent beautiful or wealthy orphan girls from marrying others, offer them an unfair, low mahr (dower), and cause them harm. The verse prohibited this practice.\n\nThe ruling of the verse is the prohibition of marrying more than four women. However, although some Zahiris claimed that the sum of these numbers is nine, this view contradicts scholarly consensus (ijma). On the other hand, the Rafidis went to extremes by asserting that there is no limitation whatsoever. What is correct is that the maximum number permitted by the verse is four. Those who fear that they cannot maintain justice must be content with a single woman.\n\nIn the subsequent verses, it is commanded that women be given their mahr graciously as a gift. It is enjoined not to surrender wealth to those of weak judgment (safihs), but rather to provide for their maintenance from it and speak to them with kind words. Orphans must be tested until they reach the age of marriage and demonstrate sound judgment (rushd), and only then should their property be handed over to them. Guardians must not squander orphans' property; if a guardian is wealthy, he should abstain from it entirely, and if he is poor, he may consume of it within lawful and reasonable limits.\n\nRegarding the rulings of inheritance, it is declared that men and women have a designated share in what parents and close relatives leave behind. This verse abolished the custom of the pre-Islamic period of ignorance (Jahiliyyah) that denied inheritance to women and children. Revealed upon the complaint of a woman named Umm Kuhla, this verse establishes that inheritance does not belong exclusively to men. Subsequently, the amounts of the prescribed shares (fara'id) are explained in detail.\n\nIf relatives, orphans, and the needy are present during the division of inheritance, it is recommended to give them something from it and speak to them kindly. In addition, those who fear leaving weak children behind them are urged to show the same sensitivity toward orphans. A stern warning is given that those who unjustly consume the property of orphans are only filling their bellies with fire and will enter Hell. Following these warnings, people avoided intermingling with orphans, but this fear was alleviated by a subsequent verse in Surah al-Baqarah.\n\nAllah commands you concerning your children regarding the division of inheritance: for the male, a share equal to that of two females; if there are only daughters numbering more than two, they receive two-thirds of the estate; if there is only one daughter, her share is half. For the parents of the deceased, each receives a sixth of the estate if the deceased left children; if he left no children and his parents are his heirs, his mother receives a third; if he left siblings, his mother receives a sixth. These shares are applied after the payment of any bequests and debts. Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.\n\nThe inheritance of spouses: if she has no child, half of the wife's estate belongs to the husband, and if she has a child, one-fourth. From the husband's estate, if he has no child, one-fourth belongs to the wife, and if he has a child, one-eighth. If there are siblings who inherit through kalalah (having left neither ascendants nor descendants), a single brother or sister receives a sixth; if they are more than that, they share equally in one-third. All shares are distributed after the payment of any bequests and debts. Allah is All-Knowing, Most Forbearing.\n\nThese two verses regulate the system of inheritance: the first addresses those with direct lineage, such as children and parents, while the second addresses indirect ties, such as spouses and kalalah (collateral heirs). During the Jahiliyyah, inheritance was based on combatant men and contractual alliances; women and children could not inherit. Islam initially continued this system with additional grounds such as the Hijrah and brotherly pacts, and later, through al-Anfal 75, retained only the grounds of kinship (nasab), marriage, and wala' (the bond of emancipation).\n\nThe occasion of revelation of the inheritance verse: When Sa'd ibn al-Rabi' was martyred, he left behind two daughters, a wife, and a brother. When the brother took the entire estate, the wife complained to the Prophet (peace be upon him). Thereupon, this verse was revealed and the first division of inheritance was carried out: two-thirds was given to the daughters, one-eighth to the mother, and the remainder to the brother. This initiated a momentous transformation, establishing women and children as legal heirs and recognizing marriage as a legal basis for inheritance.\n\nThe wisdom behind a male receiving the share of two females: The financial burden of maintenance (nafaqah) in the family falls upon the man; a man spends on both himself and his wife, whereas a woman spends only on herself. For this reason, the man's share is larger. Furthermore, a woman's financial liability is generally less than that of a man; this distinction is also observed in matters such as blood money (diyah). Islam observes justice by ensuring this balance. According to an explanation narrated from Ja'far al-Sadiq, this rule is rooted in the wisdom of creation and is an eternal law of divine justice.\n\nDetails regarding the inheritance of children: An only son receives the entire inheritance; in mixed genders, a male receives twice the share of a female. The share of two daughters is two-thirds (except for Ibn Abbas, who states that it is half). If there are children, the father and mother each receive one-sixth; if there are no children, the mother receives one-third and the father takes the remainder. If there are siblings, the mother's share drops to one-sixth. All shares are distributed after debts and bequests; bequests come after debts (as Ali pointed out).\n\nKalalah: This refers to kinship outside of ascendants and descendants; it comes into play when the deceased leaves neither children nor parents. The siblings meant in this verse are maternal siblings; for this reason, they receive equal shares. The rulings concerning other siblings will appear at the end of the surah. The concept of kalalah was debated among the Companions; while Abu Bakr stated that it refers to relatives other than parents and children, Umar indicated that it meant leaving no offspring.\n\nIn the verse of inheritance, Allah Almighty commanded that bequests and debts be settled in a manner that does not harm the heirs. The Prophet (peace be upon him) stated, 'There is no bequest for an heir,' indicating that this ruling was abrogated by the inheritance verses. A bequest cannot exceed one-third of the estate; anything beyond that is subject to the permission of the heirs. Falsely acknowledging a debt during a deathbed illness is also deemed causing harm to the heirs, and such debts are not paid from the inheritance.\n\nThe limits ordained by Allah: Whoever obeys Allah and the Messenger will be admitted into Gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein forever. Whoever disobeys and transgresses the limits will be cast into Hell, facing a humiliating punishment. It was commanded that four witnesses be brought against women guilty of unlawful sexual intercourse, and once the offense was established, that they be confined to their homes until death; however, this ruling was abrogated by the verse of flogging in Surah an-Nur. It was also ordained that male and female offenders be subjected to reprimand and distress, which was subsequently transformed into the prescribed legal punishment.\n\nThe repentance acceptable to Allah is only that of those who commit sin in ignorance and promptly repent. The repentance of those who say 'I have repented' at the moment of death, or those who die as disbelievers, is invalid. As for the condition of those who persist in sin and repent shortly before the death agony, it is subject to Allah's will. A believer's repentance at the final breath may be valid, yet this has not been definitively promised.\n\nIt is unlawful to inherit women against their will or to coerce them in order to recover part of the bridal gift (mahr) given to them, unless they commit open indecency. You must live with women in kindness; even if you dislike them, Allah may have placed much good in them. When divorcing a wife to marry another, the mahr given to the former must not be reclaimed. Marrying women whom one's fathers had married in the pre-Islamic era of ignorance (Jahiliyyah) is strictly prohibited.\n\nAllah has forbidden you to marry the following women: your mothers, daughters, sisters, paternal aunts, maternal aunts, nieces (daughters of brothers and sisters), foster mothers, foster sisters, mothers-in-law, stepdaughters from wives with whom you have consummated the marriage, the wives of your own biological sons, and two sisters joined in marriage at the same time. Those prohibited through fosterage are the same as those prohibited through blood relations. Marrying free married women is also forbidden, except for female captives of war. Marriage was instituted for the purpose of chastity and reproduction; temporary marriage (mut'ah) contracted solely for pleasure is impermissible.\n\nIn verse 25, it is stated that those lacking the financial means to marry free believing women may marry believing slave women with the permission of their masters. It is stipulated that these slave women must be chaste, neither engaging in unlawful intercourse nor taking secret paramours. If they commit illicit intercourse after marriage, they are subject to half the punishment prescribed for free women. While Imam al-Shafi'i regarded this situation as forbidden, Imam al-A'zam considered it disliked (makruh). Marrying a slave woman is presented as an alternative for those who fear falling into sin; nevertheless, exercising patience is better.\n\nIn verses 26–28, Allah's purpose in establishing these rulings is made clear: to teach you what you did not know, to guide you to the ways of those before you, and to accept your repentance. Furthermore, it is highlighted that those who follow their lusts seek to divert people far from the straight path, whereas Allah desires to lighten your burdens. Man is reminded that he was created weak, and it is affirmed that Islam is a religion of ease. These verses serve as an answer to the supplication in Surah al-Fatihah, 'Guide us to the straight path.'\n\nIn verse 29, the believers are instructed not to consume one another's wealth unjustly, while trade conducted by mutual consent is declared lawful. The term 'batil' (falsehood/vanity) encompasses all unlawful means of acquisition and expenditure, such as theft, breach of trust, usury (riba), and gambling. Trade is explicitly mentioned because it represents the primary and most prevalent lawful basis of ownership. In addition, it is emphasized that the requirement of mutual consent must be respected in commercial transactions.\n\nAgain in verse 29, the expression 'do not kill yourselves' bears multiple meanings: committing suicide, exhausting one's soul with unbearable hardship, causing one's own death through crime, or placing oneself in danger. It also prohibits taking one another's lives. Unjustly devouring wealth, hoarding, and illicit profiteering can likewise bring about people's deaths. Out of Allah's mercy, such unlawful acts have been forbidden, while lawful trade has been granted freely.\n\nIn verse 30, it is stated that whoever commits these transgressions out of injustice and enmity will be cast into the fire of Hell. That is effortless for Allah. Following this, in verse 31, glad tidings are given that those who avoid the major sins will have their minor faults expiated and will be admitted into an honorable station. In verse 32, people are reminded not to covet that by which Allah has favored some over others, for each has a portion of what they have earned. In verse 33, heirs are designated, and the instruction is given to distribute the shares to those with whom oaths of allegiance were made.\n\nIn the 31st verse, Allah proclaims that He will pardon the minor sins of those who refrain from major sins and will admit them into a pleasant dwelling, Paradise. This is a manifestation of Allah's mercy. In the recitation of Nafi' and Abu Ja'far, reading the letter 'mim' with a fatha, it bears the meaning 'We will enter you into an honorable, delightful place of hospitality.' In this context, it is emphasized that one must not covet the rank of another and must avoid envy and malice, as these inevitably lead to major sins.\n\nIn the 32nd verse, coveting the blessings that Allah has granted more abundantly to some over others is forbidden. Such longings signify envy, malice, and discontent with Allah's decree. Men have a share of what they have earned, and women have a share of what they have earned; each receives the reward of their own striving. The true obligation is not to desire another's portion, but to labor using the capacities bestowed by Allah and to ask of Him. Concerning the occasion of revelation of this verse, it is narrated that Umm Salamah voiced a desire for women to receive shares of inheritance equal to those of men.\n\nIn the 33rd verse, the rules of inheritance are summarized: for every man and woman, there is an appointed share left by parents, close kin, and those bound by solemn covenants. This is a grace from Allah, and no one has been left solely to their own earning. Therefore, do not covet one another's portions and do not consume wealth unjustly; surely Allah is a witness over all things.\n\nIn the 34th verse, it is affirmed that men are caretakers and guardians (qawwamun) over women. This position rests upon two foundations: Allah having favored some above others in creation, and men providing support from their wealth. In instances of women's rebellious discord (nushuz), a progression of measures is outlined: first exhortation, then separating beds, and finally striking lightly. The occasion of revelation was an incident in which Sa'd ibn al-Rabi' struck his wife; while the Prophet (peace be upon him) initially intended to grant equal retaliation (qisas), this verse was revealed, underscoring the man's authority over the woman.\n\nIn verse 35, in the event of discord, it is commanded that an arbitrator be sent from each of the two families. There is disagreement regarding the authority of the arbitrators; some say they have the authority to separate, while others say they can only reconcile. The verse shows that the primary objective is reconciliation; Allah reconciles between those who seek reconciliation with good intentions. The disobedience of men will be addressed later in verse 128.\n\nIn verses 36–41, ten important duties are listed: worshiping Allah, being good to parents, and doing good to relatives, orphans, the needy, the near neighbor and the distant neighbor, the companion, the traveler, and slaves. Allah does not love those who are arrogant, stingy, and enjoin stinginess upon others. The occasion of revelation of these verses is that certain Jews advised the Ansar not to spend their wealth on the Emigrants (Muhajirun). Furthermore, it is stated that on the Day of Resurrection a witness will be brought from every community and the prophets will testify.\n\nIn verses 42–43, it is explained that on the Day of Resurrection the disbelievers will desire to be swallowed up by the earth, yet they will not be able to conceal anything. Next comes the prohibition against approaching prayer: prayer cannot be performed while intoxicated or in a state of major ritual impurity (junub), unless one is passing through on a journey, without taking a full ritual bath (ghusl). If one is ill or traveling and cannot find water, dry ablution (tayammum) is permissible. The words 'junub', 'gha'it', and 'lamastum' are explained; there is a difference of opinion between the Hanafi and Shafi'i schools regarding the nullification of ablution by touch.\n\nThe word tayammum means \"to intend,\" and intention is part of the essence of this act of worship. \"Sa'id\" means the earth's surface; it encompasses stone and soil. For this reason, even if soil does not stick to the hands, tayammum with a stone is permissible. However, Imam al-Shafi'i stated that at least a little soil must cling to the hands. \"Tayyib\" means pure and clean; therefore, it must not be impure or doubtful. Cleanliness is so important in Islam that when water cannot be found, one must form the intention, direct one's heart toward purification, and wipe the face and hands up to the elbows with clean earth. This demonstrates that both physical and spiritual purification are united. Allah is Pardoning and has granted a concession for tayammum.\n\nIn verses 44–46, the Jews who were given a portion of the Scripture are criticized. They distort words from their original meanings and, twisting their tongues, say, \"We hear and disobey,\" \"Listen, may you not hear,\" and \"ra'ina\" (look at us). Yet, had they said, \"We hear and obey,\" \"Listen,\" and \"look upon us,\" it would have been better for them. Allah cursed them on account of their disbelief. Distortion (tahrif) occurs in three ways: altering words, misinterpreting them, and uttering statements deceptively. These verses reveal their disrespect toward the Prophet and their obstinacy.\n\nIn verse 47, Allah calls the People of the Book to believe in the Quran, which confirms the Torah in their hands. Otherwise, their faces will be effaced and turned backward, or they will be cursed just as the Sabbath-breakers were cursed. In verse 48, it is stated that Allah will never forgive associating partners with Him, but He will forgive other sins for whomsoever He wills. This identifies associating partners with Allah (shirk) as the greatest sin. The claims of the Jews, such as \"We are the sons of Allah and His beloved,\" are rejected.\n\nVerses 49–55 describe those who claim purity for themselves and the Jewish leaders who regarded the idolaters as superior to the believers. Huyayy b. Akhtab and Ka'b b. al-Ashraf allied with Quraysh, prostrated before idols, and disparaged the Muslims. Verses 51–55 emphasize that they believed in idolatry and taghut (false deities/tyranny), and that they are those whom Allah has cursed. In verses 56–57, it is stated that as for the disbelievers who deny the verses, whenever their skins are roasted through in Hell, their skins will be renewed; whereas those who believe and perform righteous deeds will abide eternally in Gardens beneath which rivers flow, blessed with purified spouses and dense shade.\n\nVerse 58 is a very important verse in which Allah commands returning trusts to their rightful owners and judging between people with justice. Trust (amanah) means trustworthiness; it is of three kinds: toward Allah, toward people, and toward oneself. This verse was revealed concerning a well-known incident during the conquest of Mecca regarding the return of the key of the Kaaba to Uthman b. Talha. One must comply with Allah's commands and avoid betrayal and injustice.\n\nIn verse 59, believers are commanded to obey Allah and the Prophet, and to obey those in authority among them. However, this obedience is not unconditional; one does not obey someone who commands sin. The qualification \"among you\" indicates that obedience to non-believing rulers is not obligatory. While believers should not revolt, they must not obey in matters that involve disobedience to Allah. Regarding this, our Prophet stated: \"A Muslim is obligated to listen and obey, whether he likes it or dislikes it, except when commanded to commit a sin; if commanded to sin, there is no obedience.\"\n\nIn verse 83 of Surah an-Nisa, it is commanded that news of security or fear should not be spread immediately, but instead brought to the Prophet and those in authority. This indicates the existence of scholars and jurists who have the capacity to derive rulings from the verses. Fakhr al-Din al-Razi explains that these people of authority are the \"ahl al-hall wal-aqd\" (those qualified to bind and loosen), that is, the people of consensus (ijma), and that their agreement represents the community (ummah), forming an evidence after the Quran and Sunnah. Regarding the occasion of revelation of the verse, it is narrated that during a detachment under the command of Khalid b. al-Walid, Ammar b. Yasir granted protection (aman) to a man, leading to a dispute with Khalid. The Prophet (peace be upon him) accepted Ammar's protection, but reminded him not to give pledges on his own against his commander again.\n\nBelievers are commanded to refer any dispute that arises among them to Allah and His Messenger. This means not judging solely according to one's own desires, and turning not to other authorities, but first to Allah and the Prophet. In Islam, rulings are of four types: those explicitly stated in the Quran, those stated in the Sunnah, those established by consensus (ijma), and those derived through sound analogical reasoning (qiyas). Qiyas is the deduction of a ruling in issues not clarified in the sacred texts (nass) by seeking similarity and the effective cause (illah). Although the Zahiri school rejects qiyas, it is in fact impossible to resolve disputes without qiyas. In the event of disagreement, recourse is made to the consultation (shura) of the authorities and ultimately to the ruler, which is itself obedience to Allah's command.\n\nThe verses draw attention to the hypocrites who claim that they believe: they wish to resort to taghut (the transgressing Satan who does not fear Allah). Yet they were commanded to reject taghut. According to a narration, a hypocrite and a Jew had a dispute; the Jew wanted to go to the Prophet (peace be upon him), while the hypocrite wanted to go to the leader of the Jews, Ka'b b. al-Ashraf. When Umar heard that the hypocrite was not satisfied with the judgment of the Messenger of Allah, he killed him, and this act was praised in the sight of Allah. Thus, this incident demonstrates that resorting to the court of taghut is disbelief, and that true faith requires complete submission to the judgment of the Prophet.\n\nThose who obey Allah and the Messenger will be together with the prophets, the truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous. Following this comes the command to fight directed to the believers: all kinds of precautions must be taken against the enemy, and they should mobilize either in small detachments or all together. However, those among them who drag their feet rejoice if a calamity befalls them, and if a victory is won, they express regret saying, \"If only I had been with them.\" The truly sincere fight in the cause of Allah, selling the life of this world for the Hereafter; whether they are killed or achieve victory, they will receive a great reward. The legitimacy of war is to rescue the oppressed and weak men, women, and children. Believers fight in the cause of Allah, while disbelievers fight in the cause of the taghut (false deities); indeed, the plot of Satan is weak.\n\nThose who were once restrained from fighting and commanded to establish prayer and give zakah began to fear people as they would fear Allah when fighting was made obligatory upon them. The enjoyment of this world is brief, and the Hereafter is far better for the righteous. Death will reach you wherever you may be; even if you are in fortified towers, you cannot escape. Good is from Allah, while evil is from one's own self. Words such as \"thubat\" (a troop of more than ten people) and \"fatil\" (the thin thread in the groove of a date stone, signifying an infinitesimal amount) are explained. Obedience to the Messenger is obedience to Allah; you have not been sent as a keeper over those who turn away. The hypocrites outwardly say \"we obey,\" yet when night falls, they conspire other things. The Quran should be pondered; had it been from other than Allah, they would surely have found many contradictions in it.\n\nAs narrated in this section, when the Prophet (peace be upon him) arrived in Medina, there was initially abundance and affordability. However, when the obstinacy of the Jews and the hypocrisy of the hypocrites surfaced during the call to Islam, famine and high prices set in. This situation was a trial also witnessed in communities to which earlier prophets had been sent. The hypocrites and the Jews attributed these afflictions to the Prophet, saying, 'This man brings misfortune; since he arrived, crops have dwindled and prices have soared.' These verses were revealed precisely in response to their slanders. The phrasing of the verse encompasses not only this specific incident, but also general circumstances such as victory or defeat in war.\n\nIn the continuation of the verse, it is proclaimed: 'Say: Whatever good comes to you is from Allah, and whatever evil befalls you is from your own self.' This statement emphasizes that everything occurs by the decree of Allah, yet evils afflict humans on account of their own faults or sins. A person should recognize all good as coming from Allah, and ascribe evil to oneself. Regarding the question of destiny (qadar), this verse accepts neither strict fatalism (jabr) nor absolute autonomy; it teaches a balance between the two. The will of Allah encompasses all things, yet human responsibility remains intact.\n\nAfter stating that obeying our Prophet is obeying Allah, it is recounted how the hypocrites secretly hatch plans of defiance. Outwardly they say, 'at your command,' yet as soon as they leave your presence, they conspire by night to do the exact opposite of what they told you. Allah knows and records whatever they conceal. What falls upon our Prophet is solely conveying the message; he is not tasked with being their guardian. For this reason, the believers must rely upon Allah in every matter and pay no heed to the schemes of the hypocrites.\n\nAs proof that the Quran is the word of Allah, it is underscored that it contains no contradiction or inconsistency whatsoever. Had it originated from other than Allah, they would have found many discrepancies and contradictions within it. On the contrary, the Quran is in perfect harmony, both in its historical reports and in its legal rulings. As for the apparent differences between verses, they are arranged with divine wisdom according to changing times and circumstances. For this reason, believers should contemplate the Quran and realize that it originates from the presence of Allah.\n\nThe believers are instructed not to spread rumors the moment they hear them, but to consult the Prophet and those in authority first. In this manner, the accuracy of the report and its suitability for the public interest must be examined. Those possessing the capacity for istinbat (deduction), that is, ijtihad (independent legal reasoning), can derive the proper judgment regarding such events. This verse also teaches that uninformed individuals must refer matters to scholars. Moreover, Umar's investigating the rumor that the Prophet had divorced his wives and uncovering the truth is among the reasons for the revelation of this verse.\n\nThe command to fight in the cause of Allah serves as a reminder that an individual is responsible for his own soul. The Prophet (peace be upon him) was resolute in marching out to battle even if he were left entirely alone at the Battle of Badr Minor (Badr al-Sughra). The believers must be urged to battle, for Allah will surely restrain the might of the disbelievers. Whoever engages in a good intercession (mediation) partakes in its reward, and whoever engages in an evil intercession bears a share of its burden. For this reason, believers should mediate for good and strictly avoid mediating for evil.\n\nThe etiquette of greeting is likewise taught here: when you are greeted with a greeting, return it with one that is better, or return it equally. In Islam, the greeting of peace (salam) is a supplication for safety and well-being in both this world and the Hereafter. The greetings of the pre-Islamic period of ignorance (Jahiliyyah), such as 'May God grant you a long life,' are deficient; the authentic greeting consists of wishing peace and security. This verse also points toward being prepared for peace after war. Muslims should reciprocate accordingly whenever enemies lean toward peace.\n\nDetailed explanations are provided regarding offering and returning the greeting of salam. Salam is among the beautiful names of Allah, and in twelve instances in the Quran, Allah greets the believers. Returning a greeting is a collective obligation (fard al-kifayah). In situations such as listening to a sermon (khutbah), reciting the Quran, or teaching sacred knowledge, greetings are not answered. It is Sunnah for the one walking to greet the one sitting, the rider to greet the pedestrian, the younger to greet the elder, and the smaller group to greet the larger group. That the person giving the greeting should not raise his voice excessively has been narrated from Imam al-A'zam (Abu Hanifah). A given greeting must be returned at least in kind, and kindness must not be left unreciprocated.\n\nIt is affirmed that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that He will gather everyone together on the Day of Resurrection (verse 87). Our being divided into two factions concerning the hypocrites is reproached; Allah has cast them back because of the sins they earned. It is impossible to guide the one whom Allah has left to stray (verse 88).\n\nThe hypocrites wish that you too would fall into disbelief; they must not be taken as allies until they emigrate in the cause of Allah, and if they turn away from emigration, they must be seized and killed wherever they are found (verse 89). However, those who seek refuge with a people with whom you have a treaty, or those who remain neutral, fighting neither you nor their own people, are exempt from this ruling. Had Allah willed, He could have given them power over you; if they offer you peace, attacking them is impermissible (verse 90). There are also others who seek to be secure from both you and their own people, yet whenever they are lured back into discord (fitnah), they plunge headlong into it; if they do not withdraw from you nor offer you peace, they must be seized and slain (verse 91).\n\nIf a believer kills another believer by mistake, as an expiation (kaffarah) he must emancipate a believing slave and pay blood money (diyah) to the family of the deceased, unless the family charitably foregoes it. If the slain person was a believer but belonged to a people hostile to you, only the emancipation of a slave is required; if he belonged to a people with whom you maintain a treaty, both blood money and expiation are incumbent. Whoever cannot find a slave to free must fast for two consecutive months (verse 92). As for the penalty for one who intentionally murders a believer, it is eternal Hellfire; Allah is wrathful with him, has cursed him, and has prepared a tremendous punishment for him (verse 93).\n\nWhen setting out for jihad in the cause of Allah, thorough investigation must be made to distinguish the believer from the disbeliever. To anyone who offers a greeting of peace or surrenders, one must not say, 'You are not a believer,' in order to pursue worldly gain. With Allah there are abundant spoils. You too were like that before, and Allah bestowed His favor upon you. Therefore, investigate thoroughly and do not act hastily. Allah is well aware of what you do (verse 94).\n\nIn this section, certain incidents encountered by the believers during war are recounted. For example, a Companion named Mirdas, despite having accepted Islam, was killed by Usamah ibn Zayd; the Prophet (peace be upon him) was deeply grieved by this and rebuked Usamah, saying, \"How could you kill him when he had said la ilaha illallah?\" Similarly, Muhallam ibn Jussamah, who killed 'Amir ibn al-Adbat, met with the same reaction and passed away shortly thereafter following the Prophet's supplication against him. These incidents demonstrate that a person who professes to be a Muslim must not be killed immediately and that intentions must be left to Allah.\n\nEven if a person who declares that they have entered Islam is insincere, this declaration must prevent them from being killed. Indeed, al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad questioned the Prophet regarding a similar situation, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"Do not kill him! For if you kill him, he will be in your position after you kill him, and you will be in his position before he uttered the word he said.\" These hadiths emphasize that even in war, killing someone who professes to be a Muslim is a grave sin, and that unless one cuts open their heart, their faith cannot be questioned.\n\nFollowing these incidents, verses 95–96 of Surah an-Nisa were revealed. In these verses, it is stated that those who remain behind from battle without a legitimate excuse and those who strive (jihad) in the cause of Allah with their wealth and their lives are not equal. Allah has favored the mujahideen over those who sit back by a degree and promised them a great reward. However, Paradise has been promised to both groups. The concept of 'excuse' encompasses physical impediments such as illness, blindness, and lameness, or the lack of necessary provisions for battle. These verses indicate that jihad is a collective obligation (fard al-kifayah) and that not everyone bears the same degree of responsibility.\n\nVerses 97–100 address those Muslims who did not emigrate and remained under oppression. As the angels take the souls of those who wrong themselves, they ask them, \"What was your state?\" and they reply, \"We were oppressed in the land.\" The angels reject their excuse, saying, \"Was Allah's earth not spacious enough for you to emigrate?\" However, men, women, and children who are truly helpless and destitute are exempt from this ruling; it is hoped that Allah will pardon them. Emigration (hijrah) is, when necessary, a form of jihad, and an individual must depart from a place where they cannot practice their religion.\n\nIn verse 100, it is proclaimed that whoever leaves their home emigrating in the cause of Allah and then dies along the way, their reward is incumbent upon Allah. This underscores the significance of intention and effort. As in the case of Jundub ibn Damrah, whoever sets out with the intention of emigration and passes away on the way receives a reward as though they had reached their destination. This verse dispels the apprehensions holding Muslims back from emigration and recalls Allah's forgiveness.\n\nVerses 101–104 explain how prayer is to be observed during travel and in states of fear. When traveling upon the earth, there is no blame in shortening the prayer if harm from the disbelievers is feared. When the Prophet (peace be upon him) led his Companions in prayer during battle, one group would stand in prayer carrying their weapons while the other group stood guard; then the groups would alternate. In instances of rain or illness, it is permissible to lay down weapons, yet vigilance must not be abandoned. Once fear has subsided, prayer is performed in full. One must not show slackness in pursuing the enemy, must bear the suffering they cause with patience, and must place hope in Allah.\n\nThese verses demonstrate that even in arduous circumstances such as war and emigration, acts of worship must not be neglected, yet dispensations are provided according to circumstances. Furthermore, the sincerity of intention and the imperative of trust in Allah are emphasized, aiming to embolden the believers concerning both jihad and emigration. In every circumstance, Allah knows the condition of His servants and shows them mercy.\n\nAccording to Hanafi scholars, shortening the prayer during travel is performing the obligatory prayer in its original two-rak'ah form; for when prayer was initially made obligatory, it was ordained as two rak'ahs during travel, and was subsequently expanded to four rak'ahs when resident. Therefore, the \"shortening\" mentioned in the verse pertains to quality/manner, encompassing conditions such as praying by gesture (ima') or while walking in times of fear.\n\nThe manner of performing the fear prayer (salat al-khawf): The imam divides the congregation into two groups; while one group prays, the other keeps watch against the enemy. After the first group completes one rak'ah, they yield their place to the second group, who then pray the second rak'ah behind the imam. Each group then completes their remaining rak'ah independently. In this manner, everyone performs two rak'ahs.\n\nIn the verse, it is commanded to keep weapons at hand and remain vigilant so that the enemy cannot launch a surprise raid. The incident of Ghawrath ibn al-Harith recounts the Prophet's rescue through Allah's help under such circumstances. This incident shows that taking precautions and placing trust in Allah must be undertaken together.\n\nPrayer has been made obligatory at designated times. Allah has appointed five states in the rising and setting of the sun as markers for these times: noon, afternoon, evening, night, and dawn. These times have been compared to the stages of human life; each time serves as an occasion for gratitude and servitude to Allah.\n\nWhen the battle concludes and a secure atmosphere is established, prayers must be performed in full or those that were missed must be made up. Furthermore, weakness must not be exhibited against the enemy, bearing in mind that they too suffer pain; nevertheless, one must never deviate from justice, and the rights of the disbelievers must be respected.\n\nThe occasion of revelation for verses 105–115 is the incident of Banu Ubayriq. An individual named Tu'mah ibn Ubayriq falsely accused a Jewish man of theft; however, when the truth became evident, Allah admonished the Prophet not to defend the treacherous. These verses emphasize that one must never be an advocate for the treacherous and that justice must be administered equally to all.\n\nVerses 106–108 articulate the gravity of judicial authority and the solemnity of the responsibility borne by judges, witnesses, and advocates. While Allah Almighty protects the Prophet from error—stemming from his inclination to defend Tu'mah on the basis of outward Islam and the entreaties of his clan—He indicates that for a judge to rule in favor of a traitor, even unwittingly by being deceived by apparent pretexts, constitutes a shortcoming. For this reason, refraining from defending the treacherous and seeking Allah's forgiveness is enjoined. Allah is Forgiving (Ghafur) and Merciful (Rahim) toward those who seek His pardon. This warning conveys a severe admonition and an urge to repentance directed at those who knowingly support traitors, those who instigate injustice out of self-interest or partisanship, and judges who follow their own personal opinion and desires.\n\nBetraying oneself is a person's deceiving oneself and exposing oneself to punishment by committing sins. Siding with a traitor is likewise a betrayal of one's own self. Allah does not love anyone who is sinful and treacherous. In this expression, there is a warning against persistence and habitual sin, as well as an encouragement toward repentance. Furthermore, some of those who betray themselves utter words with which Allah is not pleased, and while doing so, they have no fear of Allah, seeking only to hide from people. The word 'tabyit' means to deliberate and scheme by night, or to craft speech like a poetic verse (bayt). These individuals secretly concoct evil schemes, make decisions contrary to Allah's pleasure, and seek to deceive people. Yet Allah encompasses all things and will fully requite what they do.\n\nIn verse 109, addressing those who defend the traitors, it is said: \"Here you are—you are the ones who argued on their behalf in the life of this world; but who will defend them on the Day of Resurrection?\" Through this defense in the world, not only do they fail to save them, but by partaking in their guilt, they betray their own selves. In verses 110–111, whoever commits an evil deed or wrongs himself and then asks Allah for forgiveness will find Allah Forgiving and Merciful. However, whoever commits a sin earns it only against his own soul, and bears its harmful consequences himself. Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.\n\nIn verse 112, it is stated that whoever commits an error or a sin and then casts it upon an innocent person has burdened himself with slander and a manifest sin. There are three different views regarding the distinction between 'khati'ah' (error) and 'ithm' (sin): first, khati'ah is a minor sin, while ithm is a major sin. Second, khati'ah is a minor sin whose effect remains confined to the doer, whereas ithm is a transitive sin that wrongs another person. Third, khati'ah is that which is improper to do whether committed deliberately or inadvertently, whereas ithm is that which is committed intentionally. Slander is itself a major sin; shifting one's own sin onto someone else is to add the sin of slander onto that sin. Therefore, it is necessary to avoid such conduct, to repent, and to seek forgiveness and make amends with the wronged party.\n\nIn verse 113, it is stated that had it not been for Allah's grace and His mercy, a faction of the traitors would have sought to mislead the Prophet. Although the kin of Tu'mah knew he was a thief, they came to the Prophet asking him to defend him and to pin the theft on a Jewish man. Yet they were only leading themselves astray. Allah revealed the Book and wisdom to the Prophet and taught him secrets he did not know. This is a tremendous grace protecting him from error both outwardly and inwardly. In verse 114, it is stated that there is no good in most of their secret counsels, except for those conducted for three purposes: charity, kindness, or reconciliation between people. These three terms encompass all righteous deeds: procuring benefit (charity / sadaqah), averting harm (reconciliation / islah), and spiritual benefit (righteous conduct / ma'ruf). For whoever does these seeking the pleasure of Allah, there is a great reward.\n\nIn verse 115, it is stated that whoever opposes the Messenger after guidance has become clear to him and follows a path other than that of the believers, will be left to what he has chosen and cast into Hell. This verse was revealed concerning Tu'mah, who fled to Mecca and apostatized after his treachery was exposed. 'Shiqaq' means to break away and enter into defiant opposition. The way of the believers is the path of tawhid (monotheism) and the sound religion. Opposing the Messenger of Allah means straying from the path of the believers, and this demonstrates that adhering to the consensus of the Ummah (ijma' al-ummah) is obligatory. In verse 116, it is reiterated that Allah does not forgive associating partners with Him (shirk), but forgives anything less than that for whom He wills. Shirk is both a monstrous fabrication and a profound deviation. The shirk of the People of the Book is predominantly based on fabrication, whereas that of others is predominantly based on ignorance. Neither will be forgiven unless one repents.\n\nIn verse 117, it is stated that those who associate partners with Allah invoke none besides Him except female entities (inath). Most of their idols had female names and female shapes. In the polytheists' conception of god, the image of woman is predominant; they view their deities not as active, authoritative beings, but as female entities that yield to them. Through the deception of Satan, this deviance extends even to the worship of woman. Actual women, on the other hand, are degraded by this conception. The polytheists' supplication is a form of worship in which affection and hope predominate. Gods of fear, on the other hand, are generally male, and worship of them takes the form of groveling flattery.\n\nIn verses 118–120, it is recounted that the defiant Satan was cursed by Allah and swore that he would take a designated portion of His servants, mislead them, fill them with false desires, command them to slit the ears of livestock, and command them to alter Allah's creation. What is meant here is every kind of deviance brought about through the whisperings of Satan: such as making women resemble men and men resemble women, using bodily organs outside their purpose of creation, and declaring the lawful to be unlawful and the unlawful to be lawful. These five declarations of Satan constitute the head of shirk and the fountainhead of deviance.\n\nIn this section, mention is first made of the deceptive promises of Satan, who turns people away from truth and goodness. Satan is an accursed being who rebelled against Allah's command. Those who take him as an ally are enticed by his promises and deluded by false pride. Yet all of Satan's promises are nothing but an illusion that will only bring disappointment to humanity. The ultimate destination of those who follow Satan in this manner is Hell, from which there is no escape. However, those who repent and turn away from shirk retain the hope of forgiveness.\n\nIn contrast, for those who believe and perform righteous deeds, Gardens beneath which rivers flow have been promised. This promise is Allah's absolute pledge, and who is more truthful in speech than Allah? Paradise cannot be attained through the wishful thinking of the Muslims nor through that of the People of the Book. Each person can attain Allah's mercy only by doing good and guarding against evil. Not even the slightest injustice will be done, and everyone will receive the complete recompense for their deeds.\n\nThe best religion in the sight of Allah is that of one who surrenders his whole being to Allah, does good, and follows the way of Abraham (Ibrahim) as a hanif (an upright monotheist). For Allah took Abraham as an intimate friend (khalil). This friendship is an exalted station attained through Abraham's devotion and sacrifices for Allah. Those who follow his path also partake of this friendship. This religion is Islam, and there is none better than it.\n\nIt is mistaken for Christians to regard the expression 'son of God' as a badge of honor and to claim on that basis that Christianity is superior. Ascribing a son to Allah is associating partners with Him and constitutes a grave slander. Furthermore, a master's son is not necessarily superior to his servant; the son of Noah is an example of this. True superiority lies in friendship with Allah and servitude to Him. Even the Gospel itself corroborates this truth.\n\nWhatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth belongs to Allah. He encompasses all things. Neither the friendship of Abraham nor the curse of Satan lies outside Allah's dominion. Everyone is subject to Allah's authority and decree. Man must exercise his free will in accordance with Allah's commands, judge with justice, and seek His good pleasure in every matter. This is the purpose of creation and the reason for the sending of the scriptures.\n\nFollowing these explanations, answers are given to rulings (fatwas) requested concerning women. Matters such as withholding the inheritance rights of orphan girls and being unwilling to marry them are addressed. Allah commands acting with justice in these affairs. Asking the Prophet for a ruling is intended to ascertain the correct judgment in difficult issues. A mufti must be qualified and trustworthy.\n\nIf a woman fears ill-treatment or estrangement from her husband, there is no blame upon them in making peace between themselves through reconciliation. Reconciliation is better than separation. Souls are inclined to selfishness; however, living together amicably and guarding against discord is pleasing in the sight of Allah. An agreement can be reached through the woman relinquishing some of her rights or the man making a sacrifice.\n\nFull justice between wives in terms of love is not possible, for the heart is not subject to one's will. However, equality must be observed in material rights such as maintenance and overnight stays. It is not permissible to incline completely toward one wife and leave the other suspended. If injustice is avoided and things are rectified, Allah is All-Forgiving.\n\nIf the husband and wife decide to separate as a result of disagreement, Allah will enrich each of them out of His vast bounty, leaving them in no need of the other. Separation must be with the mutual consent of both parties; coercion or injustice is a sin. Allah is the Owner of whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth; everything is in need of Him, while He is in need of nothing.\n\nAllah has commanded both the previous communities and the Muslims to fear Him. Let those who disbelieve know that whatever is in the heavens and on the earth belongs to Allah. He is worthy of all praise and does not need the worship of His servants. People's disbelief causes Him no harm, nor does their gratitude bring Him any benefit. He merely protects His servants out of His mercy and guides them to the right path.\n\nAllah Almighty informs that, if He wills, He can eliminate you and bring another people in your place. Upon the revelation of this ayah, it is narrated that the Messenger of Allah tapped the back of Salman al-Farsi and said, 'They are his people.' Whoever desires the reward of this world should know that the reward of both this world and the Hereafter is with Allah; the wise person should ask for both rewards or prioritize the Hereafter. Allah knows intentions and repays everyone according to their intention.\n\nO you who believe! Stand firmly for justice, bearing witness for Allah alone, even if it is against yourselves, your parents, or your close relatives. Whether rich or poor, Allah is closer to both of them than you are. Do not follow the desire of your soul and deviate from justice. If you distort your testimony or refuse to give it, Allah is fully aware of what you do.\n\nBelieve in Allah, His Messenger, the Book He sent down to His Messenger, and the scriptures sent down before. Whoever denies Allah, His angels, His books, His messengers, and the Last Day has fallen into deep misguidance. Those who believe, then disbelieve, then believe again, then disbelieve, and then increase in disbelief—Allah will neither forgive them nor guide them to the right path. There is a painful punishment for the hypocrites; they take the disbelievers as allies instead of the believers, whereas all honor and glory belong entirely to Allah.\n\nAllah has already revealed to you in the Book that when you hear His verses being denied and ridiculed, you must not sit with those disbelievers unless they engage in another topic; otherwise, you will be just like them. Indeed, Allah will gather all the hypocrites and disbelievers together in Hell. The hypocrites keep watch over you; if a victory comes to you, they say, 'Were we not with you?' but if the disbelievers achieve a victory, they say, 'Did we not gain advantage over you and protect you from the believers?' Allah will judge between you on the Day of Resurrection, and He will never grant the disbelievers a way over the believers.\n\nThe hypocrites seek to deceive Allah, yet Allah will turn their plots back upon them. When they stand up for prayer, they stand lazily, showing off, and they remember Allah but little. They waver between disbelief and faith, belonging neither to the believers nor to the disbelievers. Whoever Allah leaves to stray, no way of salvation can be found for him. O you who believe! Do not take the disbelievers as allies instead of the believers; otherwise, you will give Allah a clear proof against yourselves. The hypocrites are in the lowest depth of Hell, and you will find no helper for them. Except those who repent, make amends, hold firmly to Allah, and are sincere in their religion—they will be with the believers. Allah will grant a great reward.\n\nAllah does not like offensive speech to be uttered openly, except by one who has been wronged. Whether you reveal a good deed or conceal it, or pardon an evil, Allah is All-Forgiving and Omnipotent. Those who disbelieve in Allah and His messengers desire to differentiate between them by believing in some and disbelieving in others. They are the true disbelievers; a humiliating punishment has been prepared for them. As for those who believe in Allah and His messengers and make no distinction between any of them, Allah will soon grant them their rewards. Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\n\nThere are those who disbelieve in Allah and His messengers, those who separate Allah from His messenger in faith, and those who accept some messengers and reject others. The Jews and Christians belong to this last group. This verse demonstrates that there is no middle path between faith and disbelief; rejecting one prophet is equivalent to rejecting them all, and rejecting all of them is equivalent to denying Allah. For this reason, Allah has prepared a humiliating punishment for all disbelievers.\n\nAs for those who believe in Allah and His messengers and make no distinction between any of them, Allah will give them their rewards and forgive their sins. This promise is for the believers.\n\nThe People of the Book asked our Prophet to bring down a book to them from heaven. Yet they had asked Moses (Musa) for something even greater, saying, 'Show us Allah openly.' Because of their wrongdoing, they were struck by lightning; then they worshipped the calf. Allah pardoned them and gave Moses a clear proof.\n\nAllah raised Mount Tur over them to take their pledge, commanded them to enter the gate prostrating, told them not to violate the Sabbath, and took a firm covenant from them. Yet they broke their pledge, rejected the verses, killed the prophets, and said, 'Our hearts are wrapped.' For this reason, Allah sealed their hearts.\n\nThe Jews were cursed on account of their disbelief in Jesus and their uttering a monstrous slander against Mary, as well as their saying, 'We killed the Messiah, Jesus, son of Mary, the messenger of Allah.' Yet they neither killed him nor crucified him, but it was made to appear so to them. Those who differ over this matter are in doubt about it. Allah raised Jesus up to Himself.\n\nEvery one of the People of the Book will surely believe in Jesus before his death. On the Day of Resurrection, Jesus will be a witness against them. Because of the wrongdoing of the Jews, their taking of usury, and their consuming people's wealth wrongfully, Allah forbade them the wholesome things that had previously been lawful to them, and prepared a painful punishment for the disbelievers.\n\nThose among the People of the Book who are well-grounded in knowledge and the believers believe in what has been revealed to you and what was revealed before you. They establish prayer, give zakat, and believe in Allah and the Last Day. For them, there is a great reward.\n\nAllah has revealed to you just as He revealed to Noah and the prophets after him. He also revealed to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, the Descendants, Jesus, Job, Jonah, Aaron, Solomon, and David. He sent messengers as bringers of good tidings and warners so that mankind would have no excuse before Allah. Allah bears witness to what He has revealed to you. Those who disbelieve and hinder others from the path of Allah have strayed into deep misguidance. Allah will neither forgive them nor guide them to the right path; they will abide in Hell forever.\n\nRevelation (wahy) is of several kinds: seeing Gabriel (Jibril) and hearing the speech, hearing a voice from behind a veil, breathing into the heart, inspiration (ilham), subjugation (taskhir), and by means of true dreams. Literally, wahy is a swift and hidden communication. In its general sense, it can apply to anyone; however, as a technical term, it refers to the special communication from Allah to the prophets. This special revelation imparts definitive knowledge, and prophethood begins only with such revelation.\n\nAllah revealed to many prophets after Noah: Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, the descendants, Jesus, Job, Jonah, Aaron, Solomon, and He gave the Psalms (Zabur) to David. None of these prophets had a book sent down all at once, as claimed by the People of the Book. The Psalms is a book containing no legal rulings, consisting solely of wisdom, admonition, and praise.\n\nProphets do not consist solely of those who are known. Allah sent many more messengers whose names were mentioned as well as those whose names were not mentioned. For this reason, the claim that prophets emerged only from Palestine and its surrounding regions is invalid. Allah's choice is based on divine wisdom; this circumstance should not be regarded as strange, just like characteristics in other matters. The exact number of prophets is not known; only those necessary for faith have been made known.\n\nAllah addressed Moses (Musa) directly with speech from behind a veil, which is the highest rank of revelation. Other prophets were also inspired at various levels. That prophets received revelation in different ways casts no doubt upon their truthfulness. The gradual descent of the Quran also rests on a divine wisdom; for the People of the Book to demand a book brought down all at once from heaven is baseless.\n\nAllah Himself bears witness to the Quran He sent down to His Messenger; the angels also bear witness. This is a miracle sent down with divine knowledge. Those who disbelieve in it and obstruct people from the path of Allah are in deep misguidance. It is not possible for them to be forgiven or guided to the right path; they shall abide in Hell forever.\n\nO people! The Messenger has brought you the truth from your Lord; believe in him for your own good. O People of the Book! Do not go to extremes in your religion and do not say anything about Allah except the truth. The Messiah, Jesus son of Mary, is the messenger of Allah, His word conveyed to Mary, and a spirit from Him. Do not say, 'Allah is three'; that is better for you. Allah is One, exalted above having a child; to Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth.\n\nJesus does not disdain from being a servant to Allah, nor do the angels. Whoever shows arrogance against servitude, Allah will gather everyone before Him; He will grant those who believe and do good deeds their reward in full and more, while He will punish those who act arrogantly with a severe torment. Allah is sufficient to set the affairs of His servants right; seeking a guardian other than Him is futile.\n\nThere has come to you from your Lord a clear proof (Muhammad) and a manifest light (the Quran). Those who believe in Allah and hold fast to Him, He will admit them into His mercy and grace, and guide them to a straight path.\n\nThey ask you for a legal ruling concerning the inheritance of a kalalah (a person who leaves behind neither parent nor child). Say: Allah gives you a ruling as follows: If a person dies without children and has a sister, half of his estate belongs to her. If a sister dies and has a brother, the brother inherits from her. If there are two sisters, two-thirds belongs to them. If the siblings are a mixture of male and female, the male's share is equal to the share of two females. Allah makes this clear to you lest you go astray; and He is All-Knowing of everything.\n\nJust as when a brother dies, if a sister dies leaving behind neither child nor father, the brother inherits her entire estate. But if the sister has a son or a father, the brother cannot inherit; if she has a daughter, he cannot take the whole estate, but takes what remains.\n\nUnder the same conditions, if there are two or more sisters, their prescribed share is two-thirds of the estate. If there is a remainder, it is given to the 'asabah (agnatic/paternal relatives); if there are none, it reverts to the sisters by way of radd (reversion).\n\nIf the surviving siblings are a mixture of male and female, the male is given a share equal to the share of two females. Allah explains these rulings to you lest you go astray.\n\nThe Basran scholars interpreted the expression 'lest you go astray' in this explanation as 'disliking that you should go astray,' whereas the Kufan scholars interpreted it as 'so that you do not go astray.' In our dialect, saying 'lest you err' carries the same meaning; that is, if it is not explained, you would err. However, presuming an implicit negative particle is better than presuming a conditional clause.\n\nThis verse is an answer to a request for a legal ruling that begins with the phrase 'They ask you for a ruling,' and serves as a preparation for the explanations in Surah al-Ma'idah. Allah knows all things; knowing your circumstances concerning life and death, He has made clear the rulings that are for your benefit. You, too, should learn them and walk on the straight path."